## Assembler Directives

Directives are commands to the assembler (not the CPU). They control where code/data is placed in memory.

| Directive      | Example                    | Description |
|----------------|----------------------------|-------------|
| `ORG addr`     | `ORG 2000H`               | Set the origin — next instruction/data goes at this address |
| `DB data`      | `DB 05H, 0AH, 0FH`       | Define byte(s) — store raw data in memory |
| `DW data`      | `DW 2050H`                | Define word — store 16-bit value (low byte first) |
| `DS count`     | `DS 10H`                  | Define storage — reserve `count` bytes of memory |
| `EQU value`    | `COUNT EQU 05H`           | Equate — assign a constant value to a label |
| `END`          | `END`                     | End of source code |

### Labels

Labels mark addresses in your program for jumps, calls, and data references:

```asm
LOOP:                      ; Define a label
    DCR C                  ; Decrement counter
    JNZ LOOP               ; Jump back to LOOP if not zero
```

- Labels end with a colon (`:`).
- They can contain letters, digits, and underscores.
- They must start with a letter or underscore.

